  "nl_text": "The database schema named 'rschema_0352' consists of five tables: 'Supplier', 'Customer', 'Product', 'Invoice', and 'Invoice_Product'.\nThe 'Supplier' table represents a supplier entity with attributes such as 'Supplier_Address', 'Supplier_ID', 'Supplier_Name', and 'Supplier_Phone'. The 'Supplier_ID' is the primary key for this table.\nThe 'Customer' table represents a customer entity with attributes such as 'Address', 'Customer_ID', 'Email', 'First_Name', 'Last_Name', and 'Phone'. 'Customer_ID' is the primary key for this table.\nThe 'Product' table represents a product entity with attributes such as 'Description', 'Minimum_Stock_Quantity', 'Name', 'Product_ID', 'Purchase_Price', 'Selling_Price', 'Stock_Quantity', and 'Supplier_ID'. 'Product_ID' is the primary key and 'Supplier_ID' is a foreign key referencing the 'Supplier' table.\nThe 'Invoice' table represents an invoice entity with attributes such as 'Customer_ID', 'Date', 'Invoice_ID', 'Tax_Amount', and 'Total_Value'. 'Invoice_ID' is the primary key and 'Customer_ID' is a foreign key referencing the 'Customer' table.\nFinally, the 'Invoice_Product' table represents a many-to-many relationship between invoices and products. It has attributes such as 'Invoice_ID', 'Product_ID', and 'Product_Quantity'. 'Invoice_ID' and 'Product_ID' are the primary key and 'Invoice_ID' and 'Product_ID' are foreign keys respectively referencing the 'Invoice' and 'Product' tables.",
  "gold_question": "Retail Management System\n\n1. Supplier Management\nRecord the basic information of suppliers, including supplier ID name, address, and phone number.\nYou can query and manage supplier information.\n\n2. Customer Management\nRecord the basic information of customers, including customer ID, first name, last name, phone number, address, email, etc.\nYou can query and manage customer information.\n\n3. Product Management\nRecord the detailed information of products, including name, description, purchase price, selling price, inventory quantity, and minimum inventory quantity.\nBy associating supplier information with supplier ID, you can track the supplier of the product.\nYou can query and manage product information, including inventory management.\n\n4. Invoice Management\nRecord the detailed information of each invoice, including invoice ID, date, tax amount, total value, and customer information.\nBy associating customer information with customer ID number, you can track the customer of the invoice.\nYou can generate and manage invoices.\n\n5. Invoice Product Association Management\nRecord the products and quantities contained in each invoice.\nAssociating invoice and product information with invoice ID and product ID.\nYou can query the products and quantities contained in each invoice, as well as the detailed information of the products.",
  "input_json": "/data/RSchema_ExpResults4/Experiment2/schema2text_deepseek/0352.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: rschema_0352\nCREATE TABLE `Supplier` (\n  `Supplier_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Supplier_Name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `Supplier_Address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Supplier_Phone` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Supplier_ID`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Customer` (\n  `Customer_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `First_Name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `Last_Name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `Address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Phone` varchar(15) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Email` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Customer_ID`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Product` (\n  `Product_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `Description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Purchase_Price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `Selling_Price` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  `Stock_Quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Minimum_Stock_Quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Supplier_ID` int(11) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Product_ID`),\n  CONSTRAINT `product_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`Supplier_ID`) REFERENCES `Supplier` (`Supplier_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Invoice` (\n  `Invoice_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Customer_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `Tax_Amount` decimal(10,2) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `Total_Value` decimal(10,2) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Invoice_ID`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoice_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`Customer_ID`) REFERENCES `Customer` (`Customer_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `Invoice_Product` (\n  `Invoice_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Product_ID` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `Product_Quantity` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`Invoice_ID`, `Product_ID`),\n  CONSTRAINT `invoice_product_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`Invoice_ID`) REFERENCES `Invoice` (`Invoice_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `invoice_product_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`Product_ID`) REFERENCES `Product` (`Product_ID`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
